  "body": "## What this PR does\n\nThis PR is the release branch of CamCraft 0.27.0. It merges to `main` when the cut gate (`cut.yml`, stage gate) is green on its head and the review is clean. Then the tag stage tags the merge commit.\n\nThe branch starts from `main` at 5b52c5f8c (process bump PR #4090). It has these commits:\n\n1. `docs: 0.27.0 release notes; changelog`:\n   - `cargo xtask decisions --version 0.27.0` moves 126 decision records into `RECOMMENDED.md`.\n   - `RELEASE_NOTES.md` has the 0.27.0 section. \"Breaking\" is first.\n   - `CHANGELOG.md` has 112 rows for the PRs merged since v0.26.0.\n2. `chore(release): 0.27.0 - release` and `chore(release): sync Cargo.lock to 0.27.0`: the version bump from `cargo xtask release --version 0.27.0 --no-tag --skip-gate`.\n   - Phases 1-7 passed. The post reports 0.27.0.\n   - Phase 8 (local Linux packaging) stopped: `package-linux: missing rpmbuild`. This failure does not undo the bump commit. The local zip is only a hand-test build. `release.yml` builds the published packages from the tag.\n   - The tool writes a fixed `Co-Authored-By` trailer. Before the push, the two commits were made again with the same trees and the trailer of this session.\n\n## Cut list\n\nThe cut line is 2026-11-25T19:17Z. The maintainer gave the list in the question dialog.\n\nIn the cut:\n- PR #3970 (drill order), PR #3980 (sinker picks), PR #3997 (Face picks), PR #4002 (wire lead-in).\n- The owed process bump 154 -> 155 for PR #4043 and PR #4049 (PR #4090).\n- Always in the cut: a fix for a red main or a red release gate (PR #4048 merged).\n\nRemoved from the cut by the maintainer: PR #4009 (dovetail corner radius). Issue #4004 goes under \"Known issues\".\n\nOut of the cut: #4050, #4014, #4008 and all other items.\n\n## Breaking\n\nThe project file format goes from v78 to v82:\n- v79: the taper tip form (#3368, PR #3929).\n- v80: the 2D Chamfer tip offset (#3153, PR #3251).\n- v81: the stated rotary axis (#2772, PR #3674).\n- v82: Rotary Flow (#2826, PR #3999).\n\nA tool library file can say `.camtools` 5 (a drawn cutter, PR #3906) or 6 (a tip form, PR #3929). An operation with start or exit picks must be generated again one time (#3339, PR #3871).\n\n## Notes for the reviewer\n\n- **Not run:** the Windows pixel-snapshot lanes and the Windows arms of the per-OS pinned readings. No Windows machine was available. `RELEASE_NOTES.md` says this in \"Pixel snapshots\".\n- **PR #3997:** the pr-test run on the PR runner ran 887 of 933 steps. It stopped at its 90-minute limit with no FAIL row and no STALL row. These runs cover the other steps:\n  - A full local run of 933 steps by another reviewer at 46747abe0 (932 passed). The UI lib step was red there because there was no GPU adapter.\n  - Our xvfb run of the UI lib (1688 passed).\n\n  The cut gate runs the full tree before the tag.\n- **PR #4060** (post-merge workflow, `.github`) is not on the cut list. It merged at 19:56Z. It is on `main`, and thus it is in the tagged tree. It changes no product code.\n- **Changelog rows:** 154 PRs merged since v0.26.0. 111 rows come from the `Changelog:` lines of the PRs. PR #3499 had no `Changelog:` line. Its row comes from the PR description (the five-axis approach order, #3414). 39 PRs said `none`: docs, tests, process, CI, build, and internal or test-only changes. Three process bump PRs (#3761, #3778, #3779) had no line, and they need no row. Two of the `none` PRs change a message or a lock. They were not shipped before, so they have no row:\n  - PR #3115: the refusal of a collapsed open chamfer run.\n  - PR #4048: the release of the UI link lock.\n\n## Known issues\n\nThese open `P1` and `P2` issues are not on the cut list. `RELEASE_NOTES.md` lists each of them. Two `P1` issues are important:\n- #4031 (P1): a tool has no cutter hand, so a left-hand drill or mill runs M3.\n- #4004 (P1): dovetail cutters ignore `corner_r`. Its fix, PR #4009, was removed from this cut.\n\nA PR comment lists all of the open `P1` and `P2` issues.\n\nChangelog: none (release mechanics)\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
